All true AFAIK, but it's a big step from saying "the world is made up
of elements" to the goofy-looking tidal wave with a face in the old
AD&D Monster Manual.  Or am I remembering Tulane University's mascot
"the Green Wave" here?  The two were pretty similar, if I recall
correctly..

There's also things like the undead.  Chinese has a word "gui" which
means "ghost".  Ghosts, devils, demons, monsters in general are "gui".

Then you have goblins, orcs, kobolds, hobgoblins, ogres, and trolls,
all of which elicit basically the same set of ideas and adjectives
depending on which stories you've read.  Generally they're ugly,
stinking, pointy-toothed bad guys.  How can we come up with half a
dozen different chinese translations?

Perhaps things can be translated literally, with long names that
explain what they are, but it might be better to instead change them to
creatures that can be understood without reference to AD&D.  (For
example, orcs might be "pig-men" and kobolds "dog-men".)



